1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:08,900
Robot checkpoints are cheap to save but expensive to evaluate, and even an accurate AI judge can be confidently wrong.

2
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:16,825
Cosmos-PLV picks which robot checkpoint to test next, and shows when its AI judge can be trusted.

3
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:27,475
PLV screening, rollouts and NVIDIA Cosmos run on one local GPU; NVIDIA Nemotron runs on Nebius Token Factory.

4
00:00:28,167 --> 00:00:39,167
The dashboard recommends what to test next, beside the free latest-epoch baseline and each rule's measured regret, and warns when strong checkpoints may fool the judge.

5
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:46,658
Cheap screening re-runs the original PLV metric script, live, with no rollouts.

6
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:52,272
Shortlisted checkpoints run in a real robosuite simulation.

7
00:00:52,272 --> 00:00:55,925
Cosmos reports, frame by frame, where the can is.

8
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:02,022
This is a live call to NVIDIA Cosmos3-Edge on the local GPU.

9
00:01:02,022 --> 00:01:09,100
It reads eight keyframes, and a fixed rule turns its answers into grasp, drop or success.

10
00:01:10,067 --> 00:01:15,369
Now the same video goes to NVIDIA Nemotron on Nebius Token Factory.

11
00:01:15,369 --> 00:01:21,217
The live verdict comes back with its request ID, latency and token count.

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,075
The results tab scores both judges against verified simulator outcomes on 330 recorded rollouts.

13
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:39,725
Nemotron was right on 96.4% of them, but it still called 9 of 67 failures a success.

14
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:51,175
Exploratory analyses: adaptive allocation, not PLV, cut rollouts two- to threefold; a time limit saved 16% of rollout time.

15
00:01:51,867 --> 00:01:53,673
But can we trust the judge?

16
00:01:53,673 --> 00:01:55,681
Our pre-registered audits say:

17
00:01:55,681 --> 00:01:56,417
not always.

18
00:01:56,417 --> 00:02:03,042
The judges could not tell when a replan helped, and they accept failures that end in the goal bin.

19
00:02:04,133 --> 00:02:07,077
Here the can is in the wrong compartment.

20
00:02:07,077 --> 00:02:08,970
The simulator says failure.

21
00:02:08,970 --> 00:02:11,633
Nemotron says it was placed correctly.

22
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:13,763
It is systematic:

23
00:02:13,763 --> 00:02:27,450
Nemotron let 58 of 149 failures into a self-training set, and the resulting policy reached 75% success, versus 86% with plain continued training.

24
00:02:28,133 --> 00:02:33,183
About 7,000 Nebius calls cost an estimated five dollars.

25
00:02:33,867 --> 00:02:36,327
Faster robot evaluation matters.

26
00:02:36,327 --> 00:02:44,017
But before trusting AI-generated labels to train robots, audit what the judge systematically misses.

